<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=keplers-laws">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kepler's laws of planetary motion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1609">1609</a>–<a href="/years/1621">1621</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Kepler's laws</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kepler's laws of planetary motion give good approximations for the orbits of planets around the Sun. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a> published them from 1609 to 1621 in three works, Astronomia nova, Harmonice Mundi and Epitome Astronomiae Copernicanae, using the accurate observations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a>. The first law, that a planet's orbit is an ellipse with the Sun at one focus, appeared in 1609 and the third law, relating the orbital period to the semi-major axis, in 1619, while the area law took its modern form in 1621. They replaced the circular orbits and epicycles of Copernicus's heliostatic model. Newton's law of gravitation, published in 1687, allows the third law to be derived for circular orbits.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By 1700 most leading astronomers accepted that the Earth moves around the Sun, and Newton’s physics, united with <b>Kepler’s laws</b>, explained the motions of the heavens and of bodies on Earth by the same mathematical principles.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
